        <div id="what-is-almond-protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is Almond Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almond protein powder is almonds with most of the oil taken out. Whole almonds are about 21 percent protein and about half fat by weight. Pressing removes much of the oil, and milling what is left produces a defatted almond flour that is roughly 45 to 55 percent protein. A technical sheet from the Almond Board of California gives a range of about 41 to 54 g of protein per 100 g for almond protein powder, averaging about 50 g, with 9 to 11 g of fat, and notes that figures vary by supplier.
          </p>

          <p>
            That process is the reason almond protein differs from most plant protein powders. Pea, rice and soy isolates are usually made by wet extraction, dissolving and precipitating the protein to reach 80 to 90 percent purity. Almond protein is closer to a whole food: Bob's Red Mill describes expeller pressing with no chemical solvents. You keep the fiber, magnesium and some of the almond flavor, and you avoid the sodium that often comes with extracted isolates. You also get fewer grams of protein per scoop.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Almond protein vs almond flour vs powdered almonds</h3>

          <p>
            The naming is messy. Regular almond flour is ground blanched almonds with the fat left in, at about 20 to 25 percent protein, and is not a protein powder. Almond protein powder is the defatted version. Products sold as powdered almonds, such as Naked Almond, are also defatted, which is why Naked describes it as more than 55 percent protein. Some products sit in between. The quick test is to divide grams of protein by the serving weight in grams: above about 0.4 is a real almond protein; around 0.2 is almond flour.
          </p>

          <p>
            Almond protein also appears as an ingredient inside plant blends, usually alongside pea, rice or seed proteins. In those products it is typically a minor component listed after pea, so the blend behaves like a pea protein with some almond character. That is not a criticism; it is often the better nutrition. It just means a front label saying almond does not tell you how much almond is inside, and the ingredient order is your only clue.
          </p>
        </div>

