                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 180</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 120</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fiber</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 6 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 0-2 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sodium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 mg</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 30-60 mg</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 100-400+ mg</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Processing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pressed and milled</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Filtered from milk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually wet-extracted</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Leucine, the amino acid most closely tied to switching on muscle protein synthesis, is the next gap. Using USDA composition data for almonds, leucine makes up roughly 7 percent of almond protein, so 20 g of almond protein supplies about 1.4 g. Whey is closer to 10 to 11 percent leucine, giving about 2.5 g in a 25 g scoop. You can close that gap with a larger serving or by pairing almond with another protein, but it is a real difference for anyone using a shake to build muscle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where almond wins is the label. One ingredient, very low sodium, no gums or sweeteners in the plain versions, and no soy, dairy or pea for people avoiding those. It suits people with pea protein intolerance, which is more common than it sounds, and anyone who finds pea's earthy taste unpleasant. Our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a> guide covers the broader trade-offs, and <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> ranks the wider category.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="complete-protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Almond Protein a Complete Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Technically almond protein contains all nine essential amino acids, but not in proportions that make it a high-quality protein on its own. Its limiting amino acid is lysine, which is present in low amounts relative to human needs, so on scoring systems such as PDCAAS and DIAAS almonds rate well below whey, soy and pea. In practical terms, if almond protein were your only protein source, you would need more of it to meet your essential amino acid needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most people that does not matter, because nobody eats one protein source. Beans, lentils, dairy, eggs, meat, soy and pea are all rich in lysine, and eating a mix across the day covers the gap. It matters more if a shake is a large share of your protein, if you are vegan and relying on nuts and grains, or if you are using the shake specifically after training to support muscle growth.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the blend partner matters</h3>

          <p>
            This is where the blends earn their place, but not all partners are equal. Chickpea and pea are legumes and rich in lysine, so Wellious's almond-chickpea blend and Orgain's pea-based blend genuinely complement almond. Brown rice, the partner in PB2 Performance Almond, is also limited in lysine, so rice and almond overlap rather than fill each other's gap. Rice still adds protein, and PB2 is a perfectly good shake, but it is not the amino acid fix that a legume partner is.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you prefer Bob's Red Mill's single-ingredient powder, an easy fix is to blend it with soy milk or a scoop of pea protein, or simply to make sure the rest of the day includes legumes or dairy. Our <a href="/best-pea-protein-powder/">best pea protein powder</a> guide lists unflavored pea options that pair well, and <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> helps you set a daily target.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="almond-milk" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Can You Mix Protein Powder With Almond Milk?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, and it is the most common way people get almond into a protein shake. It is worth knowing what it adds, though. Unsweetened almond milk typically provides only about 1 g of protein per cup, because it is mostly water with a small amount of almond, so it contributes creaminess and flavor rather than protein. Calories are usually low, around 30 to 40 per cup unsweetened, which is why it is popular for weight-loss shakes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some almond milk brands sell protein-fortified versions, and these usually add pea or another plant protein rather than almond protein, so read the ingredient list if almond is the point. Sweetened and flavored almond milks can add 7 to 15 g of sugar per cup, which quickly cancels out a low-sugar powder. Many almond milks also add a little salt and gums, so if you are watching sodium, compare cartons; our <a href="/best-low-sodium-protein-powder/">best low sodium protein powder</a> guide covers that angle.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you like the taste of almond but need more protein, the most efficient combination is a plain whey isolate or pea protein shaken with unsweetened almond milk. If you avoid dairy and want almond to be the protein, Bob's Red Mill or Wellious blended with almond milk and a frozen banana hides most of the graininess. Almond-flavored whey and plant powders exist, but check the ingredient list; many use natural almond flavor rather than any almond protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            One caution: if anyone in your house has a tree nut allergy, almond protein powder and almond milk both count, even when the amounts seem tiny. Allergic reactions to almond can be severe. If you have a known or suspected nut allergy, talk to an allergist before trying almond-based products, and do not assume a blend is safe because almond is not the main ingredient.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="baking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Bake With Almond Protein Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            Baking is where single-ingredient almond protein outshines whey and pea. Because it is essentially defatted almond flour, it behaves like a flour, absorbs liquid well and browns pleasantly, without the rubbery texture whey gives baked goods or the bitterness pea can add. Bob's Red Mill suggests it for smoothies, baked goods, sauces and dressings, and recipe ideas on its page include granola bars and a dairy-free cheese-style sauce.
          </p>

          <p>
            A good starting point is to replace about a quarter to a third of the flour in a muffin, pancake or quick bread recipe with almond protein powder, then add a little more liquid, since it is thirsty. Going further tends to make baked goods dense and dry because there is no gluten. For no-bake energy balls and protein bars, almond protein can make up most of the dry ingredients, held together with nut butter, dates or honey.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which picks bake best</h3>

          <p>
            Bob's Red Mill, Naked Almond and Anthony's are all unflavored and unsweetened, so they work in savory recipes as well as sweet. The flavored blends, Wellious, PB2 and Orgain Simple, contain sweeteners and flavors that suit pancakes and muffins but not savory cooking. Remember that baking does not destroy protein in any way that matters nutritionally, so the grams on the label still count. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-baking/">best protein powder for baking</a> guide compares almond with whey, casein and egg white in recipes.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are gluten-free, almond protein is naturally free of gluten and Bob's Red Mill labels its powder gluten free; check each other product's label, since cross-contact standards vary by facility. Paleo bakers often use it too, and our <a href="/best-paleo-protein-powder/">best paleo protein powder</a> and <a href="/best-gluten-free-protein-powder/">best gluten-free protein powder</a> guides cover other fits.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-on-amazon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Buying Almond Protein Powder on Amazon: Avoid the Almond Flour Mix-Up
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common mistake when shopping for almond protein on Amazon is buying almond flour. Searches for almond protein often surface almond flour and almond meal listings from the same brands, with similar bags and similar prices. Open the label image and check the protein line against the serving weight: a real almond protein powder gives roughly 10 g or more of protein per 20 g of powder; almond flour gives about 6 g per 28 g. If the label image is missing, skip the listing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, check the seller. Below the Add to Cart button, Ships from and Sold by should show the brand or Amazon. Smaller almond protein brands are more likely to have third-party resellers, and nut-based powders go stale if stored hot or for too long, because the remaining fat oxidizes. When the bag arrives, check the lot number and best-by date and smell it: a sharp, paint-like smell means the oil has turned rancid. Keep opened almond protein somewhere cool, or in the fridge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Size and flavor traps</h3>

          <p>
            Almond protein comes in small bags, often 14 oz to 1.5 lb, so the listed price can look cheap until you work out cost per 25 g of protein. Divide the price by servings, then by protein per serving, and multiply by 25. Because protein per serving is low for some products, such as Anthony's at about 7 g per two tablespoons, a cheap bag can be expensive per gram. Check that the size and flavor selected match the price shown; variations often default to the smallest bag. Our <a href="/tools/protein-cost-calculator/">protein cost calculator</a> handles the arithmetic.
          </p>

          <p>
            Subscribe and Save typically takes 5 percent off, rising to up to 15 percent once you have five or more subscriptions in a month, which helps on a pricier niche powder. Given the shelf-life issue, set the delivery interval to match how fast you actually use a bag rather than the default monthly schedule. Our <a href="/amazon-protein-powder/">Amazon protein powder</a> guide covers more listing traps that apply across categories.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We looked for powders sold on Amazon US in which almond is a named protein source, then split them into single-ingredient almond proteins and blends. We read each label or brand page for protein, calories, fiber and sodium, and calculated protein per 100 calories. Within each group we ranked on protein per serving, the amino acid fit of any blend partner, ingredient simplicity and cost per 25 g. We excluded almond flour and almond meal, and powders that only use almond flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some almond protein figures came from retailer or database copies of labels rather than the brand, and where sources disagreed, such as PB2 Performance listing both 170 and 180 calories, we give the range. We did not test any product; texture and taste notes reflect what buyers report. Our <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> page explains how we rank products from published data.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying almond flour by accident</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Almond flour and almond meal sit next to almond protein in search results, from the same brands and in similar bags. Almond flour is about 20 to 25 percent protein. Check the label image: a real almond protein powder gives roughly 10 g of protein per 20 g of powder or more.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting whey-level macros</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Almond protein gives about 11 to 14 g of protein per 100 calories, against about 20 to 23 g for whey isolate. It is a dairy-free, whole-food choice, not a lean one. If calories are tight, an isolate or a blend with pea will serve you better.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming any blend fixes the amino acid gap</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Almond is limited in lysine. Legume partners like chickpea and pea complement it; brown rice, also lysine-limited, does not. Check the ingredient list, and if you use a single-ingredient almond protein, include legumes, soy or dairy elsewhere in your day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing it like whey</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Almond protein keeps some almond oil, which oxidizes over time, especially in heat. A sharp, paint-like smell means it has gone rancid. Buy bag sizes you will finish in a couple of months and store opened bags somewhere cool or in the fridge.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overlooking a tree nut allergy</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Almond protein, almond milk and almond-containing blends all count as tree nut exposure, even in small amounts. Reactions can be severe. Anyone with a known or suspected nut allergy should talk to an allergist before trying almond-based products, blends included.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best almond protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Bob's Red Mill Almond Protein Powder is the best single-ingredient option, with about 20 g of protein per 44 g serving per the label and solvent-free pressing per the brand. For a flavored shake, Wellious blends almond with chickpea protein for about 18 g in roughly 130 calories, per label copies at the time of writing.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is almond protein powder good for you?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can be. It is a whole-food protein with fiber, magnesium and very little sodium, and it suits people avoiding dairy, soy or pea. It is less protein-dense than whey or pea and limited in lysine, so pair it with legumes, soy or dairy elsewhere in the day if a shake is a large share of your protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in almond protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most real almond protein powders are about 45 to 55 percent protein by weight. The Almond Board of California gives 41 to 54 g per 100 g. Bob's Red Mill lists about 20 g per 44 g serving; Naked Almond about 10 g per scoop. Almond flour, by contrast, is about 20 to 25 percent protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is almond protein a complete protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It contains all nine essential amino acids, but lysine is low relative to human needs, so it scores below whey, soy and pea on protein quality measures. Blends with chickpea or pea complement it well. Eating legumes, soy or dairy elsewhere in the day also covers the gap for most people.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is almond protein better than whey?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not for protein per calorie or leucine. Whey isolate gives about 25 g of protein in 110 calories and roughly 2.5 g of leucine; almond gives about 20 g in 180 calories and roughly 1.4 g of leucine. Almond is better if you avoid dairy, want fiber, or prefer a one-ingredient whole-food powder.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can you bake with almond protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, it is one of the best protein powders for baking because it behaves like a defatted flour. Replace about a quarter to a third of the flour in muffins, pancakes or quick breads and add a little extra liquid. Unflavored versions like Bob's Red Mill also work in savory recipes and sauces.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is almond protein powder safe with a nut allergy?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Almond is a tree nut, and almond protein powder or any blend containing almond can trigger a reaction, which may be severe. If you have a known or suspected tree nut allergy, avoid these products and talk to an allergist. Check blends carefully, since almond may be a minor ingredient.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

